is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: The default gateway for Location C (router4) on interface eth0 (192.168.200.1) is misconfigured at Layer 2/3. It is running FRR RIP on the user LAN and has Linux bridge/VLAN constructs bound to the same physical port.

Mechanism: Router4’s kernel repeatedly places eth0 into bridge ports (br_vlan1/br_vlan10/br_vlan20/br_vlan50), transitioning the port through disabled/forwarding and promiscuous states. These transitions momentarily halt forwarding on eth0, so all hosts in 192.168.200.0/24 lose their gateway at the same instant for a few seconds, then recover—exactly matching the observed “all four PCs freeze together” symptom.

Localization: Router4 eth0 (link to switch4). Evidence:
- Router4 is the gateway: eth0 192.168.200.1/24 (ip addr) and user PCs default via 192.168.200.1.
- Router4 dmesg shows br_vlanX events affecting eth0: “br_vlan1: port 1(eth0) entered disabled state … entered forwarding state; eth0 entered promiscuous mode” (reconfig cycles).
- FRR is enabled on the LAN: router rip network 192.168.200.0/24 (show running-config). This unnecessarily injects routing activity into the client segment.
- Switch side forwarding tables show the gateway MAC consistently, excluding a downstream loop; pings are normally fine between outages.

Root cause: Router4’s eth0 was erroneously enslaved to multiple Linux bridges/VLANs while also running RIP on the client VLAN, causing periodic bridge state changes that cut traffic for a few seconds.

Corrective fix: On router4, remove eth0 from all Linux bridges (br_vlan*), delete any eth0.* subinterfaces tied to those bridges, and disable RIP on 192.168.200.0/24 (keep RIP only on uplinks eth4/eth5). Leave eth0 as a plain routed SVI for 192.168.200.1. After this, the gateway interface will remain continuously forwarding and the site outages will stop.
=== END ===